  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8{t}^{2}-1,1-t\u27e9\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (1-t)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{1},t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=\u27e80,1\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =\u27e8\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{1},3-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e80,1\u27e9 & =\u27e86+{C}_{1},-\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-6,t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =\u27e8\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-0-6,0-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-6,\\frac{5}{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
